Deep learning has significantly impacted graph mining, particularly in areas like node classification and graph regression. However, its application to estimating shortest path distances between nodes in graphs remains underexplored. This research shows that deep learning models can efficiently approximate these distances, even in large graphs, by leveraging vector embeddings. We propose an approach to train these embeddings using a contrastive loss function that incorporates shortest path distances. Additionally, we introduce a simplified approach that achieves comparable results while requiring less computation. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of our methods in both theoretical and practical settings, and we also provide insights into their computational efficiency. Our analysis suggests that our models can efficiently process node pairs in sublinear time, making it feasible to handle large graphs. The code used in this research is publicly available on the GraphAI platform. The results of our experiments can be accessed at a Google Drive link. See the supplementary materials for more information. A preliminary version of this research was presented at AISTATS 2021. The full version is now published in the journal Machine Learning. Our model has been deployed in a real-world application to estimate the driving distance between locations in a large map. See the appendix for more details. This work was made possible with the support of the Google Research Office. Our collaboration with Google Research is acknowledged. The authors would like to thank the support of the Google Research Office. All authors contributed equally to this research. The final version of this research was significantly improved by the addition of [Y. Chen's] significant contributions. See the acknowledgments section for more information. \end{note} 
