Formal methods for verifying properties of smart contracts have garnered significant attention, yet most existing approaches are based on object-oriented properties that can be verified using traditional methods. However, some essential characteristics of smart contracts, like ensuring correct execution, can only be assessed through the lens of "hyperproperties", which present a unique set of verification challenges. This paper introduces a new formalism for verifying these hyperproperties in smart contracts, enabling the specification of complex, higher-level behaviors. The introduced formalism is applied to the Verdi framework, which is widely used in the Coq proof assistant, and demonstrated through the verification of several authentic smart contract properties. Additionally, the framework is successfully applied to the verification of a real-world smart contract, which is shown to be vulnerable to certain attacks, and a secure patch is developed using the introduced formalism. The formalism is also compared to other verification approaches, including temporal logic and separation logic, and shown to offer significant advantages in terms of expressiveness and verification simplicity. The results demonstrate the practicality and effectiveness of the proposed framework for verifying essential smart contract properties. The formal specification and verification code is publicly available on GitHub. https://github.com/zyh1999/Verdi-Hyperproperties. 
Keywords: smart contracts, formal verification, hyperproperties, Coq, Verdi framework. 
